explanation:
transformed(value, fail, events) doubles a number or recovers to -1, then appends cleanup to the supplied list. The Error constructor takes a message; throw rejects the chain. join(",") returns a display String from the recorded entries. Await the returned promise so cleanup finishes before inspecting events.
```javascript
function transformed(value, fail, events) {
  return Promise.resolve(value)
    .then(number => {
      if (fail) throw new Error("invalid");
      return number * 2;
    })
    .catch(error => -1)
    .finally(() => { events.push("cleanup"); });
}
const events = [];
console.log(await transformed(4, false, events));
console.log(events.join(","));
```
Output: 8 then cleanup.

Omitting return from a then callback fulfills the next promise with undefined, not the value you computed and discarded. Optional practice: make the recovery callback return a different sentinel and trace the chain. This known synthetic failure policy is not a recommendation to turn every real failure into success.
