explanation:
checkEqual(actual, expected, name) compares with strict inequality, throws an Error naming a failed case, and returns true otherwise. runChecks(candidate) calls that assertion after invoking the supplied function at three boundaries. Math.min selects the smaller number and Math.max the larger, so clamp bounds its result to zero through ten.
```javascript
function clamp(value) {
  return Math.max(0, Math.min(10, value));
}
function checkEqual(actual, expected, name) {
  if (actual !== expected) throw new Error("failed: " + name);
  return true;
}
function runChecks(candidate) {
  checkEqual(candidate(-1), 0, "lower");
  checkEqual(candidate(7), 7, "inside");
  checkEqual(candidate(11), 10, "upper");
  return "passed 3";
}
console.log(runChecks(clamp));
```
Output: passed 3. The browser exercise runs these explicit checks, not Node’s test runner.

The following companion is native Node only. Save it as clamp.test.mjs. The imports load Node built-ins, not browser packages or network dependencies. test(name, callback) registers a test whose callback is executed by the runner; assert.strictEqual(actual, expected) returns undefined when equal or throws AssertionError.
```javascript
import test from "node:test";
import assert from "node:assert/strict";
function clamp(value) { return Math.max(0, Math.min(10, value)); }
test("lower boundary", () => {
  assert.strictEqual(clamp(-1), 0);
});
```
Run node --test --test-reporter=tap in that isolated directory. Node discovers the .test.mjs file and reports one passing lower boundary test. Timing lines vary. This is separate from the in-app function check.

